To allow the user to resize a shape, you can use the mouse events to track when the user is dragging the border of the shape. Here's some example code for handling the mouse events:

```javascript
let selectedShape = null;  // the current shape being resized
let resizing = false;  // is the user currently resizing a shape? let startX, startY;  // the starting position of the drag

function handleMouseDown(e) {
  resizing = true;
  selectedShape = getSelectedShape(e.clientX, e.clientY);

  // record the starting mouse position
  startX = e.clientX;
  startY = e.clientY;
}

function handleMouseUp(e) {
  resizing = false;
}

function handleMouseMove(e) {
  if (resizing && selectedShape) {
    // calculate the new size of the shape
    const deltaX = e.clientX - startX;
    const deltaY = e.clientY - startY;
    const newWidth = selectedShape.width + deltaX;
    const newHeight = selectedShape.height + deltaY;

    // update the shape's size
    selectedShape.width = newWidth;
    selectedShape.height = newHeight;

    // redraw the canvas with the updated shape
    drawShapes();
  }
}
```

In this example code, we store the currently selected shape and whether or not the user is resizing it. When the user clicks down on the border of a shape, we set the `resizing` flag and begin tracking the mouse movements. As the user drags the mouse, we calculate the new width and height of the shape based on the change in mouse position relative to the starting position. Finally, we update the shape's size and redraw it on the canvas. Rotating Shapes:
To allow the user to rotate a shape, we can use a similar approach to resizing. We'll track when the user is dragging the shape's rotation handle (which can be a small triangle or circle), and change the shape's angle accordingly. Here's some example code for handling the mouse events:

```javascript
let rotHandlePos = null;  // the position of the rotation handle
let rotating = false;  // is the user currently rotating a shape? function handleMouseDown(e) {
  rotating = true;
  selectedShape = getSelectedShape(e.clientX, e.clientY);
  rotHandlePos = getRotHandlePos(selectedShape);

  // record the starting mouse position
  startX = e.clientX;
  startY = e.clientY;
}

function handleMouseUp(e) {
  rotating = false;
}

function handleMouseMove(e) {
  if (rotating && selectedShape && rotHandlePos) {
    // calculate the angle between the rotation handle and the mouse position
    const dx = e.clientX - rotHandlePos.x;
    const dy = e.clientY - rotHandlePos.y;
    const angle = Math.atan2(dy, dx);

    // update the shape's angle
    selectedShape.angle = angle;

    // redraw the canvas with the updated shape
    drawShapes();
  }
}
```

In this example code, we again store the currently selected shape and whether or not the user is rotating it. When the user clicks down on the rotation handle, we set the `rotating` flag and begin tracking the mouse movements. As the user drags the mouse, we calculate the angle between the rotation handle and the mouse position using the `atan2` function. Finally, we update the shape's angle and redraw it on the canvas.

