```javascript
function saveImage() {
  // convert the canvas to a data URL
  const imageData = canvas.toDataURL("image/png");

  // create a link element with the data URL
  const link = document.createElement("a");
  link.href = imageData;
  link.download = "my_canvas.png";

  // simulate a click on the link element to download the image
  document.body.appendChild(link);
  link.click();
  document.body.removeChild(link);
}

function loadImage(file) {
  // create a new image object
  const image = new Image();

  // set up an event listener to detect when the image has loaded
  image.addEventListener("load", function() {
    // draw the image on the canvas
    context.drawImage(image, 0, 0);
  });

  // set the source of the image to the selected file
  image.src = URL.createObjectURL(file);
}
```

The `saveImage()` function converts the canvas to a data URL, which is a string that encodes the image data as base64. We then create a link element with the data URL and set its `download` attribute to the desired filename. Finally, we simulate a click on the link element, which triggers the file download. The `loadImage()` function loads an image file (which is passed in as a parameter) and draws it on the canvas. We create a new `Image` object and set its `src` attribute to the URL of the selected file. We then set up an event listener to detect when the image has loaded. Once the image has loaded, we draw it on the canvas using the `drawImage()` method. To call these functions, you can set up event listeners on some buttons or input elements. For example:

```html
<!-- button to save the canvas -->
<button onclick="saveImage()">Save Image</button>

<!-- input element for selecting an image to load -->
<input type="file" onchange="loadImage(this.files[0])">
``` 

These examples show a button for saving the canvas and an input element for selecting an image file to load. When the user clicks the save button, the `saveImage()` function will be called. When the user selects an image file using the input element, the `loadImage()` function will be called with the selected file as a parameter.

