```javascript
// array to store previous canvas states
const history = [];

// function to save the current state of the canvas
function saveState() {
  history.push(canvas.toDataURL());
}

// function to undo the previous canvas state
function undo() {
  if (history.length <= 1) return;

  // clear the current canvas
  context.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);

  // remove the current state from the history
  history.pop();

  // get the previous state and draw it on the canvas
  const previousState = new Image();
  previousState.src = history[history.length - 1];
  previousState.onload = function() {
    context.drawImage(previousState, 0, 0);
  }
}

// function to redo the next canvas state
function redo() {
  if (history.length < 2) return;

  // clear the current canvas
  context.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);

  // get the next state and draw it on the canvas
  const nextState = new Image();
  nextState.src = history[history.length - 2];
  nextState.onload = function() {
    context.drawImage(nextState, 0, 0);
  }

  // remove the next state from the history
  history.pop();
}
```

In this example, we maintain an array (`history`) that stores the data URLs of each state of the canvas. The `saveState()` function is called whenever the user performs an action that changes the canvas. It pushes the current state onto the `history` array.

The `undo()` function removes the current state from the `history` array and gets the previous state. It then clears the canvas and draws the previous state on the canvas using the `drawImage()` method.

The `redo()` function works similarly to the `undo()` function, except it gets the next state (i.e. The state that was removed when the user clicked the undo button) and draws it on the canvas.

To call these functions, you can set up event listeners on some buttons. For example:

```html
<!-- button to undo -->
<button onclick="undo()">Undo</button>

<!-- button to redo -->
<button onclick="redo()">Redo</button>
```

These examples show buttons for undoing and redoing actions. When the user clicks the undo button, the `undo()` function will be called. When the user clicks the redo button, the `redo()` function will be called.

Sure! Here are the steps to integrate Firebase Realtime Database with our serverless web app:

Step 1: Set up Firebase Realtime Database
In your Firebase console, go to the "Database" tab and select "Create Database". Choose a location for your database and set it to "Start in test mode" for now. Then, click "Enable". Step 2: Initialize Firebase SDK
In your "index.js" file, initialize the Firebase SDK with the following code:

```
const admin = require('firebase-admin');
admin.initializeApp();

// Access the database root
const db = admin.database().ref();
```

Step 3: Add data to the database
To write data to your database, you can create a new route in your Express app that handles "POST" requests. For example:

```
app.post('/messages', (req, res) => {
  const message = req.body.message; // Assume "message" is a parameter in the request body
  const newMessageRef = db.child('messages').push();
  newMessageRef.set({ text: message });
  res.send('Message added to database');
})
```

This code creates a new "messages" child node in the database and adds a new message to it. Step 4: Read data from the database
To read data from your database, you can create a new route that handles "GET" requests. For example:

```
app.get('/messages', (req, res) => {
  const messagesRef = db.child('messages');
  messagesRef.once('value', snapshot => {
    const messages = snapshot.val();
    res.send(messages);
  });
});
```

This code retrieves all messages from the "messages" node in the database and sends them back as a response. Step 5: Secure your database
By default, your database is in test mode which means it can be read and written to without authentication. In production, you should secure your database by setting proper database rules, which you can set in the "Database" tab of your Firebase console. That's it! You've now integrated Firebase Realtime Database with your serverless web app. You can continue to build your app by adding more routes and integrating with other Firebase services, such as Firebase Authentication or Firebase Storage.

Sure! Here are the steps to integrate Firebase Authentication with our serverless web app:

Step 1: Set up Firebase Authentication
In your Firebase console, go to the "Authentication" tab and select "Set up sign-in method". Choose the authentication methods you want to enable (e.g. Email and password, Google sign-in, etc.) and follow the instructions to enable them. Step 2: Initialize Firebase SDK
In your "index.js" file, initialize the Firebase SDK with the following code:

```
const admin = require('firebase-admin');
admin.initializeApp();

const db = admin.database().ref();

const firebaseConfig = JSON.parse(process.env.FIREBASE_CONFIG);
const clientEmail = firebaseConfig.client_email;
const privateKey = firebaseConfig.private_key;

const { UserRecordMetadata } = require('firebase-functions/lib/providers/auth');
const { FirebaseAuth } = require('firebase-admin/lib/auth');
const defaultAuth = admin.auth();
const auth = new FirebaseAuth(defaultAuth.app);
```

Step 3: Add authentication middleware
To authenticate users, you can create a middleware function that verifies the user's token before allowing them access to protected routes. For example:

```
const authenticate = (req, res, next) => {
  const authToken = req.get('Authorization').split(' ')[1];
  if (!authToken) {
    return res.status(401).send('Unauthorized');
  }

  auth.verifyIdToken(authToken)
    .then((decodedToken) => {
      req.user = decodedToken;
      next();
    })
    .catch(() => {
      return res.status(401).send('Unauthorized');
    });
};

app.use(authenticate);
```

This code assumes that the client sends an "Authorization" header with a bearer token. It verifies the token using the Firebase Authentication SDK, and adds the user ID to the request object for later use. Step 4: Create protected routes
You can now create routes that require authentication. For example:

```
app.get('/protected', (req, res) => {
  const userId = req.user.uid;
  // Do something with the authenticated user ID
  res.send('Access granted');
});
```

This code retrieves the user ID from the request object (which was added by the authentication middleware), and can be used for further processing. Step 5: Secure your app
By default, Firebase Authentication does not require authentication to read or write data to your database. If you want to enforce authentication, you can set proper database rules, which you can set in the "Database" tab of your Firebase console. That's it! You've now integrated Firebase Authentication with your serverless web app. You can continue to build your app by adding more protected routes and integrating with other Firebase services, such as Firebase Realtime Database or Firebase Storage.

Here are some basic examples of how to apply CSS blend modes:

1. Multiplying an image with a background:

```CSS
.background {
  background-image: url('background-image.jpg');
}

.image {
  background-image: url('image.jpg');
  mix-blend-mode: multiply;
}
```

2. Overlapping two images with different blend modes:

```CSS
.image1 {
  background-image: url('image1.jpg');
}

.image2 {
  background-image: url('image2.jpg');
  mix-blend-mode: overlay;
}
```

3. Applying a blend mode to text:

```CSS
.text {
  font-size: 5rem;
  color: white;
  background-color: black;
  mix-blend-mode: screen;
}
```

4. Combining a blend mode with a hover effect:

```CSS
.image {
  background-image: url('image.jpg');
  mix-blend-mode: screen;
  transition: all 0.5s ease;
}

.image:hover {
  mix-blend-mode: multiply;
}
```

Remember, the possibilities are endless when it comes to CSS blend modes. Play around with different colors, images, text, and backgrounds to create unique and visually stunning effects.

