Here is a Kotlin function that implements a depth-first search algorithm to traverse a given graph:

```
fun dfs(graph: Graph, startVertex: Vertex): List<Vertex> {
    val visited = mutableListOf<Vertex>()
    val stack = mutableListOf<Vertex>()

    stack.add(startVertex)

    while (stack.isNotEmpty()) {
        val currentVertex = stack.removeAt(stack.lastIndex)

        if (!visited.contains(currentVertex)) {
            visited.add(currentVertex)

            for (neighbor in currentVertex.neighbors) {
                if (!visited.contains(neighbor)) {
                    stack.add(neighbor)
                }
            }
        }
    }

    return visited
}
```

This function takes a `Graph` object and a starting `Vertex` as inputs, and returns a list of all visited vertices in the order they were visited.

The function maintains a `visited` list to keep track of all previously visited vertices. It also uses a `stack` to keep track of the vertices to explore, which is initialized with the starting vertex.

The `while` loop continues until all vertices in the graph have been explored. Within the loop, the function checks if the current vertex has not been visited before, and if not, it adds it to the `visited` list. It then traverses all of the current vertex's neighbors and adds them to the stack if they have not been visited before.

The function properly handles cycles in the graph to avoid infinite loops by only adding a neighbor to the stack if it has not been visited before.

